hello hi guys uh this is the making of  for minecraft the last minecart i'm  sorry you guys had to wait so long for  it we were very busy but you'll enjoy it  trust me so when we were conceptualizing  the minecraft short uh we were trying to  take like the elements of survival and  zombies and fighting your way with  monsters and kind of combine that with  like those gritty inner city zombie  movies minecraft zombie apocalypse  essentially for the costumes we actually  made them for halloween for a party  where everybody thought we were either  robots or lego dudes it was the nerd  poker halloween party i was actually a  little bit disappointed we totally out  nerded everybody at the party sam and  jimmy were minecraft dudes i made the  zombie one we kept the costumes because  we thought we'd make a video out of them  hey what do you know so we shot  minecraft in like the gritty alleys of  los angeles downtown area um there was a  graffiti pang of master chief in one of  the alleyways that we thought was pretty  appropriate so we had the pleasure of  meeting notch in person at a machinima  live stream we got to have him sign our  little diamond pickaxe that we made and  it's really awesome we got to be on the  show and meet him and talk and he's a  really cool guy he's basically a big  jolly swedish santa claus i can see why  he made minecraft so the movie opens  overlooking the los angeles landscape  but of course los angeles doesn't look  like it's actually made in minecraft so  he actually went to minecraft took all  our sweet creations and made a big map  painting out of it yeah actually as you  can see here we have a big statue nico  made we have a big bridge that brandon  made and some other great landscape  stuff was fun so we took screenshots of  those chopped them together and kind of  made a landscape out of that and dropped  that in the shot using some motion  tracking a little bit of color grading  some smoke things like that and we had a  good matte painting so the workbench  shots were pretty simple basically i  took the actual icons to use in the game  and i motion track the shot using boojoo  and drop them in as little 2d floating  layers in after effects there's a very  simple effect but it's very effective at  getting that good workbench feel so the  crater wasn't actually that hard of an  effect but it was one of people's  favorites so the first step in a show  like this is to do a 3d motion track  once again we use the program like fuji  a lot of you guys have been asking for a  tutorial we'll make one soon for the  creator all i did was model a big blocky  crater that had a good flat outline  around it and this allowed me to put the  texture of the minecraft blocks on the  inside and then for everything  surrounding it on the border i used a  matte shadow material the matte shadow  material around the crater makes it so  that even though the crater is going  underneath the ground it blocks off all  view of it unless viewed from the proper  perspective so for the zombies there's  two types of effects we did one of which  is basically just painting out faces or  arms and things like that and the other  is the full 3d zombies for the effect of  the pickaxes and all the pixelated blood  we actually had jimmy in costume there  and when we knocked off like the head in  the arm we actually just painted off the  head by replacing with some of the  background after we were done taking the  shot we'd have jimmy leave the frame get  a blank frame of just the background and  put that back in there so if we cut  anything out you just show through to  the background and we motion track the  shot so it's really easy just add a  piece of the background back into the  shot so for jimmy i covered up his arm  using a little blue splotch and i  painted out his head using the method  described earlier and when i was all  done i put pixelated blood in front of  all of it to cover up my mistakes  to pixelate the blood we just used the  mosaic filter in after effects to build  the 3d models i took pictures of the  various pieces of the costume and then  just textured that onto the 3d models it  was really easy because the guys are  just blocks i use the arch and design  material in 3ds max to texture these  costumes because it gives me a lot of  good controls over the surface  properties it makes it look really  realistic so then for when the guys get  blown up we actually use rayfire for the  physics simulation usually we use  rayfire to fragment or destroy things  but it also lets you just select objects  and have them bounce around so it's  perfect add some pixelated blood on top  of that too boom done so we actually  kind of like the effect that zombie's  getting shot so he decided to just make  a whole bunch of getting shot and made  it a bonus video yeah we felt like there  really wasn't enough of it in the actual  video so hey why not make more  so the minecraft video is the most  popular video on our channel and  actually did a huge part in helping us  grow as filmmakers on youtube so thanks  everybody for watching it a lot of  people like the music too if you guys  want to take a listen to paid in blood  by gravedigger we got a lot of  inspiration from that thanks for  watching our videos guys i hope you  learned something if you have any  questions about anything hit us up at  twitter or you can go to facebook  see you later have a good time bye  [Music]
 

